  ],
  "slug": "confidence-ordering-contextual-68b5",
  "summary": "Recent studies treat bound-state protonation, conformational landscapes, and inhibitor docking as quantitative, computable targets for drug design.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational modeling of biomolecular structure and energetics. The kinase study applies MCCE analysis to 37 inhibitor cocrystals and finds that binding shifts protonation and tautomeric states, altering bound-state energetics. The c-MYC study combines well-tempered metadynamics with all-atom MD to map a conformational landscape at the protein surface, identifying drugging pockets, and docks inhibitor 10074-G5 over 1 μs. The TP53 preprint is a small exploratory analysis: a quantum harmonic oscillator model yields von Neumann entropy along TP53, with hot spots showing higher values than non-hot regions (n=6). What is new here is treating bound-state chemical states as energetic variables rather than fixed inputs. The TP53 entry is best read as hypothesis-generating rather than validated.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-12"
}
